.TH auto-set-memory-limits 8

\" auto-man2man (used in generating subcommand lists) depends on this section
.SH NAME
.PP
auto-set-memory-limits - Set per-process virtual memory limits

\" Convention:
\" Underline anything that is typed verbatim - commands, etc.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
.nf 
.na
auto-set-memory-limits [kilobytes]
.ad
.fi

.SH ARGUMENTS
.nf
.na
kilobytes   Optional limit, otherwise 2 * physical memory
.ad
.fi

.SH DESCRIPTION

The
.B auto-set-memory-limits
command sets pre-process memory limits, causing processes that
oversubscribe memory to be terminated.  If a command-line argument
is provided, it indicates the maximum kilobytes of virtual memory a
process can use.  Otherwise, the limit is set to twice the
amount of physical RAM.

Some people argue that virtual address space is "free" on modern
systems because theoretical capacity is far beyond practical physical
memory limits, and memory isn't paged in until it
it is actually accessed.  However, allowing unlimited memory allocation
is a bad policy, as there are many buggy programs in existence that
will touch most of the memory they allocate, leading to system
thrashing or crashes.  This policy can also be deliberately exploited
by running "malloc() bombs" for denial-of-service attacks.

.SH RETURN VALUES

0 on success, non-zero error codes otherwise.

.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
.na
auto-set-memory-limits              # 2*physmem
auto-set-memory-limits 1048576      # 1 GiB
.ad
.fi

